+## 2. Finding 2 — a nonfinite measurement is a numerical failure, not a route mismatch
+
+### 2.1 The defect
+
+R4 computed:
+
+```cpp
+const bool in_bound      = std::isfinite(mt.nmse) && mt.nmse <= bound && !mt.nonfinite;
+const bool route_witness = floor_ <= 0.0 || mt.nmse >= floor_;
+```
+
+On the q8_1 route `floor_` is `1e-8`, and `NaN >= 1e-8` is false. A nonfinite measurement therefore
+made `route_witness` false and incremented `n_route_mismatch` — while the adjacent comment claimed the
+`<= 0.0` test "keeps a NaN nmse out of the route-mismatch counter". The failure stayed fail-closed;
+the attributed cause and the route ledger did not. The comment that *was* the contradiction is rewritten,
+not left standing.
+
+### 2.2 The repair
+
+Classification is now one pure function of the measurement, with three independent outcomes
+(`tests/test-backend-ops.cpp`, `sx8_ref_classify()`):
+
+```cpp
+v.nonfinite         = nonfinite_out || !std::isfinite(nmse);
+const bool in_bound = !v.nonfinite && nmse <= bound;
+v.route_mismatch    = in_bound && floor_ > 0.0 && nmse < floor_;
+v.pass              = in_bound && !v.route_mismatch;
+```
+
+Route mismatch is now defined *only* as a finite, in-bound value below the witness floor. The
+nonfinite failure is retained and independent: it fails, it is counted in a new `nonfinite` column of
+the route ledger, and the per-case line prints `NONFINITE (numerical failure; the route is not
+judged)` instead of borrowing the route-mismatch label.
+
+Two sources of "not a number" are folded in deliberately. `nonfinite_out` flags a nonfinite output
+element; `!std::isfinite(nmse)` additionally catches the case where `sx8_ref_measure()` emits
+`+INFINITY` because the output energy is zero while the error is not — finite outputs, nonfinite
+metric. That input is the one place the two flags diverge, and it must classify as numerical failure.
+
+### 2.3 Why no verdict moved, and why receipts 40–45 need no re-run
+
+**Pass-equivalence, algebraically.** When `in_bound` is true, `nmse` is finite, so
+`!route_mismatch` = `!(floor_ > 0 && nmse < floor_)` = `floor_ <= 0 || nmse >= floor_` = the old
+`route_witness`. When `in_bound` is false, both formulations give `pass = false`. The predicate for
+`pass` is unchanged on every input; only the *classification* of the failure changes.
+
+**And empirically.** Receipt 47 re-runs receipt 40's exact 24 cases on the new binary:
+
+```
+$ diff <(metric_fields 40-r4-mmvq-f16-f32twin.txt) <(metric_fields 47-r5-control-mmvq-f16-f32twin.txt)
+IDENTICAL
+```
+
+(`metric_fields` = ANSI-stripped `grep -o 'nmse=[^ ]* absmax=[^ ]* relmax=[^ ]* rms=[^ ]*'`, the same
+extraction R4 §3.1 used. Field extraction, not a whole-file diff, because 40 and 47 were written by
+different shells and so differ in encoding — see the encoding note in §4.) 24/24 cases OK in both.
+
+Receipts 40–45 are therefore left as-is rather than re-run: under `PLANT_RED` `floor_` is 0, so the
+route-mismatch counter was structurally 0 there; on the clean runs every measured `nmse` is finite and
+above the floor, so no case took the corrected branch. All six re-hash to their R4 values (§4).
+
+### 2.4 The deterministic seam that proves the classification
+
+`sx8_ref_classify_selftest()` runs once at the top of the slice, before any device work, and prints
+its result into every receipt. It is device-free, deterministic, and asserts nothing about kernels —
+it checks only the classification contract, including the three nonfinite inputs separately:
+
+| Case | nmse | nonfinite_out | bound | floor | expected |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| q8_1 in band | 1.3e-5 | false | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | pass |
+| q8_1 at floor | 1e-8 | false | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | pass |
+| q8_1 above bound | 1.0e-3 | false | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | fail, no mismatch |
+| q8_1 below floor | 1.5e-13 | false | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | fail, **route mismatch** |
+| q8_1 nmse NaN | NaN | false | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | fail, **nonfinite, NOT mismatch** |
+| q8_1 nmse +Inf | +Inf | false | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | fail, **nonfinite, NOT mismatch** |
+| q8_1 nonfinite out | 1.3e-5 | true | 5e-4 | 1e-8 | fail, **nonfinite, NOT mismatch** |
+| dequant in bound | 1.5e-13 | false | 1e-9 | 0 | pass |
+| dequant above bound | 1.0e-8 | false | 1e-9 | 0 | fail, no mismatch |
+| dequant nmse NaN | NaN | false | 1e-9 | 0 | fail, nonfinite |
+| plant, floor off | 1.5e-13 | false | 5e-4 | 0 | pass |
+
+Observed in both new receipts:
+
+```
+classify:  self-check 11/11 OK - a nonfinite measurement is a NUMERICAL failure and is
+           never counted as a route mismatch; route mismatch means a finite in-bound value
+           below the q8_1 witness floor
+```
+
+A failing self-check prints each divergence and fails the slice even when every case passed, since a
+broken classifier invalidates every verdict above it.
+
+This is what closes the R4 checker's `NOT CHECKABLE` note that the mismatch path was "structurally
+rather than adversarially exercised": the mismatch and nonfinite branches are now both exercised on
+real inputs, at the classification layer, with no device knob required.
